ing2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-35 pf-heading-7-h3">Matt</h3><h4 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-37 pf-heading-1-h3">NAPPERVILLE</h4><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-39 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Backed by 18+ years of Russo knowledge,&nbsp;Matt is well versed in equipment and product applications. His passion for turf care carries over to his&nbsp;personal life by maintaining "the best lawn on the block".</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-41 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Best Advice:</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-43 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">The best natural weed control product is a healthy, thick, and tall lawn.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="pf-c"><div data-pf-type="ContentListItem" class="sc-isRpEB gnEWqt rid-11"><div class="sc-ibQzxG cSbAnC rid-32 pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px;position:relative" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jwZJYP cIFzrt rid-33"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0691/3340/2417/files/guillermo_guardado_jr_crop_154x.jpg?v=1704202743" srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0691/3340/2417/files/guillermo_guardado_jr_crop.jpg?v=1704202743 1536w" width="240" height="240" sizes="(min-width: 1536px) 1536px, (min-width: 1024px) 1280px, (min-width: 640px) 768px, calc(100vw/3)" loading="lazy" data-pf-type="Image3" class="sc-kUdmUV QtCYW rid-34 pf-image-1"/><h3 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-35 pf-heading-7-h3">Memo<br></h3><h4 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-37 pf-heading-1-h3">HAINESVILLE</h4><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-39 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Whether you need equipment or product, JR is ready to help you figure out the best turf care plan for you.</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-41 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Best Advice:</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-43 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">ALWAYS try to get the pre-emergent fertilizer down in the spring!</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="pf-c"><div data-pf-type="ContentListItem" class="sc-isRpEB gnEWqt rid-11"><div class="sc-ibQzxG cSbAnC rid-32 pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px;position:relative" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jwZJYP cIFzrt rid-33"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0691/3340/2417/files/PHC_NB__ryan-kiedrowski_177x177_97e6f3f8-4d0e-4863-b2d7-9571f7447c80_154x.jpg?v=1688673964" srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0691/3340/2417/files/PHC_NB__ryan-kiedrowski_177x177_97e6f3f8-4d0e-4863-b2d7-9571f7447c80.jpg?v=1688673964 1536w" width="177" height="177" sizes="(min-width: 1536px) 1536px, (min-width: 1024px) 1280px, (min-width: 640px) 768px, calc(100vw/3)" loading="lazy" data-pf-type="Image3" class="sc-kUdmUV QtCYW rid-34 pf-image-1"/><h3 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-35 pf-heading-7-h3">Ryan</h3><h4 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-37 pf-heading-1-h3">NEW BERLIN</h4><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-39 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">While new to Russo, Ryan is no stranger to the OPE Industry. He is excited to bring his background to our WI store and continue learning more to best serve you!</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-41 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Best Advice:</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-43 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Preemergent fertilizer will prevent new grass seed from germinating. Take precautions or avoid newly and soon-to-be-seeded areas.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="pf-c"><div data-pf-type="ContentListItem" class="sc-isRpEB gnEWqt rid-11"><div class="sc-ibQzxG cSbAnC rid-32 pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px;position:relative" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jwZJYP cIFzrt rid-33"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0691/3340/2417/files/Brian-Wolter_LR-crop-Recovered_154x.jpg?v=1785164582" srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0691/3340/2417/files/Brian-Wolter_LR-crop-Recovered.jpg?v=1785164582 1536w" width="240" height="240" sizes="(min-width: 1536px) 1536px, (min-width: 1024px) 1280px, (min-width: 640px) 768px, calc(100vw/3)" loading="lazy" alt="Brian-Wolter_LR-crop-Recovered.jpg__PID:fb8b7905-b433-4c04-b3d1-c9944906466f" data-pf-type="Image3" class="sc-kUdmUV QtCYW rid-34 pf-image-1"/><h3 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-35 pf-heading-7-h3">Brian</h3><h4 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI rid-37 pf-heading-1-h3">SCHILLER PARK</h4><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-39 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Brian has over 15 years of experience in the turf and arbor industry. If you have any questions, he is your go to guy!</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-41 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Best Advice:</p><p class="sc-fbFjKN fqkBeE rid-43 pf-text-2" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Always ask questions!</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-section-id="pf-c518" data-pf-type="Section" class="sc-fscnvs bBcbzk pf-259_ pf-color-scheme-1"><div style="--cw:1400px" class="sc-iowYbt LsuWb"><div class="sc-ibQzxG cSbAnC pf-260_ pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px;position:relative" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jwZJYP cIFzrt pf-261_"><h3 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-bOhtQk gAzRSI pf-262_"><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Russo Lawn Care Tips</span></h3><p class="sc-bJUkdm blRcNZ pf-264_" data-pf-type="Paragraph4"><span class="pf-paragraph-content "><strong><u><span style="color: rgb(65, 117, 5);">Weed Spot Treatments
<br></span></u><u></u></strong>Spot treatment of broadleaf weeds is encouraged during summer to maintain a clean, healthy appearance.<br><br><strong><span style="color: rgb(65, 117, 5);"><u>Blanket Selective Herbicide Applications</u></span>
<br></strong>A blanket selective herbicide application involves applying a broadleaf weed control product evenly across the entire lawn—not just to visible weeds. This method is used when weed pressure is moderate to heavy or when small, young weeds are spread throughout the turf and not easily controlled with spot treatments alone.<br><br>
<strong>Why Use a Blanket Application?
<br>•</strong>	Targets hidden or newly germinated weeds that aren’t yet visible
<br><strong>•</strong>	Ensures uniform coverage, improving overall weed control results
<br><strong>•</strong>	Works well during key growth periods when broadleaf weeds are actively taking up nutrients
<br><strong>•</strong>	Improves lawn appearance, helping turf stay clean and weed free<br>
<br><strong>When do I&nbsp;Apply?</strong>
<br><strong>•</strong>	In late spring when dandelions and other broadleaf weeds are actively growing
<br><strong>•</strong>	In late summer/early fall when a second wave of weeds emerges
<br><br>These timing windows allow the herbicide to move efficiently into the plant, resulting in better control.
<br><br><strong>What does 'Selective" Mean?<br></strong>Selective herbicides are formulated to target broadleaf weeds only, such as&nbsp;Dandelions, Clovers, Plantains&nbsp;and&nbsp;Thistles without harming desirable turfgrass (when applied correctly).<br><br><u><strong><span style="color: rgb(65, 117, 5);">Crabgrass in Summer<br></span></strong></u>Peak crabgrass germination typically occurs around June 1 in the Upper Midwest and peak activity is in mid to late July. While we recommend preventing crabgrass with a spring pre emergent with Round 1 (applied 14–16 weeks earlier), there are effective post emergent products available to control existing crabgrass during summer.<br><br><u><strong><span style="color: rgb(65, 117, 5);">Watering Guidance<br></span></strong></u>Watering is one of the most important—and most misunderstood—parts of maintaining a healthy, dense, green lawn. Both underwatering and overwatering can cause stress and long term damage. Here’s a few tips&nbsp;to water correctly for strong roots and a resilient lawn.<br><br><strong>How Much Water Is Needed?<br></strong>Most lawns require about 1–1.5 inches of water per week during the growing season. This includes rainfall.
<br><strong>•</strong>	In hot, dry periods, the lawn may need a little more.
<br><strong>•</strong>	In cooler, wet periods, it may need none at all.<br>
<br><strong>When Should I Water?<br>•</strong>	Early morning (between 4 AM and 9 AM) is ideal.
<br><strong>•</strong>	Avoid evening watering, which can encourage disease.
<br><strong>•</strong>	Midday watering leads to excessive evaporation.<br><strong>•</strong> Water deeply and infrequently. This encourages roots to grow deeper into the soil.<br><br><strong>Signs that Your Lawn Needs Water<br></strong>Don't rely on a fixed schedule. Rely on signs of stress which could be:<strong><br>•</strong>	Bluish gray patches in the grass.
<br><strong>•</strong>	Footprints that remain visible after you walk across the lawn.
<br><strong>•</strong>	Wilting or folding grass blades.<br><br><strong>If You Don’t Regularly Water<br></strong>Lawns can survive periods of drought by going dormant (turning brown). Dormancy is safe, but the lawn still needs minimal water to survive.&nbsp;Apply ½ inch of water every 3–4 weeks during extended drought. This prevents the turf from thinning or dying out. Once rainfall returns, the lawn will gradually green up again.<br>
<br><strong>Avoid Overwatering</strong><br>A lawn that is overwatered often looks just as stressed as one that is underwatered.&nbsp;Too much water can cause:
<br><strong>•</strong>	Shallow roots
<br><strong>•</strong>	Increased disease pressure
<br><strong>•</strong>	Soil compaction
<br><strong>•</strong>	Weed and insect issues
<br><br><strong>Optional: Water Saving Products</strong><br>Moisture management products such as Hydretain can reduce watering needs by up to 50%. These products help the soil retain moisture and improve water efficiency during hot, dry periods.
